# Teaching guide
#
# Goal: An HTTP exchange contains two distinct messages whose methods, headers, status, and body must not be mixed together.
#
# Why this design: The compact trace makes the client/server boundary visible before any server implementation details are introduced.
#
# Follow the evidence:
# - The method and request `Content-Type` belong to the request.
# - The status and `Location` belong to the response.
# - `Example-Trace-Id` is optional extension metadata; the representation remains usable without it.
# - Both messages can carry JSON, but the bodies have different roles.

POST /api/notes HTTP/1.1
Host: course.example
Content-Type: application/json
Content-Length: 20

{"text":"Read diff"}

HTTP/1.1 201 Created
Content-Type: application/json
Location: /api/notes/n-7
Example-Trace-Id: trace-create-7
Content-Length: 31

{"id":"n-7","text":"Read diff"}
